# Summit Project - Cursor Rules

## MANDATORY Git Operations - USE AGENT GIT WRAPPER ONLY
- YOU MUST USE THE AGENT GIT WRAPPER FOR ALL GIT OPERATIONS
- NEVER use direct git commands (git add, git commit, git push, etc.)
- ALWAYS use the agent_git_api module: `from src.agent_git_api import save_work, create_pr, status, pull`
- Primary function: `save_work("commit message")` - automatically validates, tests, and pushes
- For PRs: `create_pr("commit message", "PR title", "PR description")`
- The wrapper enforces all Summit standards automatically:
  - Single-line commit messages (no line breaks)
  - No emojis in commit messages
  - Runs tests and ensures >70% coverage
  - Runs linting and pre-commit hooks
  - Monitors CI/CD status after push
- Example usage:
  ```python
  from src.agent_git_api import save_work
  # Save all changes
  save_work("Fix PR reviewer tests and improve coverage")
  # Or save specific files
  save_work("Update test configuration", ["tests/test_pr_reviewers.py", "pytest.ini"])
  ```

## MANDATORY PR-Based Workflow
- NEVER push directly to main or master branch
- ALWAYS create Pull Requests for all changes using agent git API
- Use `create_pr()` function which automatically:
  - Creates a new branch if needed
  - Runs full quality checks (tests, linting, coverage)
  - Pushes to feature branch
  - Creates GitHub Pull Request with proper formatting
  - Enables automatic merging when CI/CD passes
- Example usage:
  ```python
  from src.agent_git_api import create_pr
  create_pr(
      commit_message="Fix critical bug in authentication",
      pr_title="Fix authentication bug and improve error handling", 
      pr_description="Detailed description of changes and impact"
  )
  ```

## MANDATORY Code Quality Prevention Rules
- ALWAYS run formatting tools IMMEDIATELY after making any code changes:
  - `python -m black src/ tests/ --line-length 79`
  - `python -m isort src/ tests/ --profile black --line-length 79`
  - `python -m autopep8 --in-place --aggressive --recursive src/ tests/`
- NEVER write lines longer than 79 characters
- ALWAYS use proper import organization:
  - Standard library imports first
  - Third-party imports second (with blank line separation)
  - Local imports last (with blank line separation)
- ALWAYS check line length when writing template strings or long expressions
- BREAK long strings using parentheses and concatenation instead of backslashes
- Example of proper line breaking:
  ```python
  # GOOD
  long_string = (
      "This is a very long string that needs to be broken "
      "across multiple lines to stay under 79 characters"
  )
  
  # BAD
  long_string = "This is a very long string that exceeds the 79 character limit and causes E501 errors"
  ```

## Code Quality Enforcement
- Pre-commit hooks MUST pass before any commit
- Code formatting automatically applied (Black + isort)
- All tests must pass with >70% coverage
- Linting errors must be resolved
- No debugging statements or obvious comments allowed

## Communication Standards
- NO EMOJIS in any documentation, comments, or messages
- NO excessive celebratory language like "MAJOR SUCCESS", "AMAZING", etc.
- NO check mark symbols () or other visual decorations
- Use clear, professional, factual language only
- Report status and results objectively without emotional language
- Focus on technical facts and actionable information

## Documentation Standards
- Keep documentation concise and focused on core capabilities
- Emphasize learning and self-improvement aspects
- Use professional technical writing style
- Avoid marketing language or hype

## Code Comments
- NEVER add obvious/redundant comments like "# Test passed", "# Success", "# End of function"
- Comments should explain WHY, not WHAT the code does
- Avoid stating the obvious - let the code speak for itself
- Only comment when adding genuine value or explaining complex logic

## Testing Rules
- NO AD-HOC TESTING - Never use sleep and curl commands for testing
- FORMAL TEST SUITE ONLY - All testing must be done through pytest or proper test files
- AUTOMATED VALIDATION - Create proper test cases instead of manual command validation
- NEVER SKIP TESTS - Do not add pytest.mark.skip decorators or skip failing tests
- FIX ROOT CAUSE - Address the underlying issue causing test failures instead of avoiding them

## Development Workflow
- Follow the MANDATORY DEVELOPMENT PROCESS from CODING_STANDARDS.md
- Always run tests and coverage before committing
- Use proper Git workflow: analyze → implement → test → lint → commit → push
- Maintain >70% test coverage on all changes

## PROACTIVE QUALITY CHECKLIST
Before making any commit, ALWAYS verify:
1.  Ran automated linting fixes: `python scripts/fix_all_linting.py`
   OR manually run:
   - `python -m black src/ tests/ --line-length 79`
   - `python -m isort src/ tests/ --profile black --line-length 79`  
   - `python -m autopep8 --in-place --aggressive --recursive src/ tests/`
2.  No lines exceed 79 characters
3.  Proper import organization (stdlib, third-party, local with blank lines)
4.  No trailing whitespace
5.  All tests pass
6.  Coverage >70%